<% content_for :title, "yrby — Rails as a Yjs backend" %>

<section class="hero">
  <h1>yrby</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    yrby makes Rails a real Yjs backend. It binds
    <a href="https://github.com/y-crdt/y-crdt">y-crdt</a>, the Rust engine behind
    Y.js, into Ruby, and builds the rest of the stack around it: a sync server
    for Action Cable and AnyCable, a browser provider, and server-side reading
    and rendering of the documents. Real-time collaboration in a Rails app with
    no Node process anywhere in the path.
  </p>

  <pre class="install"><code>gem install yrby-rails # depends on yrby
npm install yrby-client</code></pre>

  <p class="cta">
    <%= link_to "Read the docs", doc_path("getting-started"), class: "button" %>
    <%= link_to "Try the demos", demos_path, class: "button button--ghost" %>
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>The whole server side is one channel</h2>
  <pre><code>class DocumentChannel &lt; ApplicationCable::Channel
  include Y::ActionCable

  on_load   { |key|         Y::Document.load_state(key) }
  on_change { |key, update| Y::Document.append(key, update) }

  def subscribed    = sync_subscribed(params[:id])
  def receive(data) = sync_receive(data, params[:id])
end</code></pre>
  <p>
    Every update is recorded through <code>on_change</code> before it is acked or
    broadcast to anyone. Replaying your store always rebuilds the document, on
    one process or a hundred.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Any Yjs shape, no editor required</h2>
  <p>
    The server treats a document as opaque CRDT state, so the same channel syncs
    a rich text editor, a spreadsheet, a whiteboard, or anything else with a Yjs
    binding. Each demo below runs against this site, on the store described in
    <%= link_to "Storage", doc_path("storage") %>.
  </p>
  <ul class="demo-list">
    <% Demos::ALL.each do |demo| %>
      <li>
        <%= link_to demo.title, demo_path(demo.slug) %>
        <code><%= demo.shape %></code>
        <span><%= demo.blurb %></span>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>This site</h2>
  <p>
    The site you are reading is a Rails app in the yrby repo
    (<a href="https://github.com/jpcamara/yrby/tree/main/site"><code>site/</code></a>).
    It runs on the published gems, has no database and no Redis, and keeps every
    demo document in process memory behind the channel's two hooks. Its README
    covers the store, the throttle design, and what it costs to host.
  </p>
</section>
